A||||5||WisconsinGT||Fuel pressure regulator||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||05-31-2000||12:09 PM||||Can someone tell me where and how I install one of these? Is it a difficult process? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||chris west||05-31-2000||12:23 PM||||Why do you want to change the fuel pressure? it won't give you any more <BR>performance only idling problems and less gas mileage. The fuel management <BR>system is is designed to work with a specified fuel pressure.<BR>Chris<BR>West Coast Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||05-31-2000||01:16 PM||||Chris, I am replacing it with a stock one. So it is already at factory specs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||05-31-2000||01:36 PM||||You need to take out only the upper plenum to access the fuel rail. This is not difficult but need to make sure you know where the vacuum lines go when assembling back. The regulator has I think 8 security torx screws. You need a special torx bit for that. They sell it at auto parts sotres. First thing you should do is disconnect battery and releive fuel pressure from system. Take out plenum and vacum lines and then regulator. I would suggest to put fuel preasure in the system before installing the plenum back just in case. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||80's BOY||05-31-2000||01:58 PM||||i've found it easier to disconnect the throttle body from the plenum. it saves time, plus you don't have to fool with the coolant lines as much. don't lose the gasket though. now is a good time for visual inspection of your vacuum lines and such. good luck!!||||reg||7
Z||000005||Alex4mula||05-31-2000||02:40 PM||||I do agree 100% with 80's on disconnecting the TBS. Forgot about that because I removed those line about 6 years ago!!||||reg||1
